\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl1800\margr1800\margt1440\margb1440\f0\fs24\cf0
\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The morning sun woke Mandri up. Groggy, hungover, he drank himself through to midday. The ox spent the morning hours feeding on his withered beanstalks. This forced him into the realization that the ox must have settled on his farm because all the other farms were protected by sturdy gates and mean dogs. Its presence was a simple matter of cause and effect\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 nothing cosmic about it. And now that he was comfortable with this development, he decided to name the ox, Gil.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 When the sun got hot, Gil rejoined him under the shade of the tree. Mandri properly wasted himself on the Burgeon hooch, and went back in for a sleep.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 His first dream was about a plate of beef. His second was a sweaty dream of about lizards. His third dream involved , and it ended when a voice inside his head said, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93This is both a surprise and completely expected.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Burgeon beer had never given Mandri an extra inner voice before.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Odd that it should sound like a woman.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Go away,\u8221\'94 he mumbled.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 But the voice did not go away. It kicked him in the shin.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ow!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pain pricked him awake. He was stunned to see a woman wrapped in black standing over him, her face shadowed by a hood. She swayed in his tree\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s shade, a void amidst the branches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri had heard of burgeonsight granting visions, but this had never happened to him. He tried to blink the woman away, but she was a stubborn vision.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He picked up a little pebble and threw it at her.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Her arm, a billow of black, flew up and caught it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She was real.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You have not honored your word,\u8221\'94 she said grimly. She tossed the stone over her shoulder. \u8220\'93Punishable.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She stepped forward. Locks of her brown hair spilled out of her hood. Waves of light slid down each strand, creating the illusion of a liquid flow. This illusion, the burgeon\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s doing, showed Mandri just how far gone he was. But he was not so far gone, not yet. He recognized that tumble of curls.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Pel?\u8221\'94 he asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel smirked. No other smirk but hers was so full of condescension. No other woman so tall. But the rest of her was someone else. She wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t adorned with the usual veneer of civilization; no shine-silver bracelets, or gold hoop earrings, or rhombic beads, no makeup, or red-threaded leaves trimming her sleeves. The only ornament on her entire body was the turtle-shell hilt of a dagger which she kept fashionably crooked in her waistband. The rest of her tawny self could have been slapped together in a reed hut on the banks of the river Jek; robes black and stained dusty brown all over. She was dirty, with perfect teeth. A full complement of perfectly straight and sun-bleached teeth were sparkling. They practically outshone the sun. She was a rude, blot of a person amidst a sparkling, burgeon addled world.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And now his legs were sweating.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Assuredly, this was Pel. A witch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 His first instinct was to drink as much of his jumped-up hooch as he could, because no doubt she would try to snatch it for herself. Then again, she might not know what it was, because it looked like milk, and besides, it would be a waste to drink it all in one go because this was a special drink, worthy of preservation. Mandri had made plenty of special drinks, but this one was extra special because it was right here, a thing of the present. He could maybe try to hide it, tuck the clay jug away somewhere, maybe just nudge it behind the tree, but no doubt that vixen\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eyes were\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re drooling,\u8221\'94 Pel said disdainfully. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Burgeons can make a mess of a person. A peculiar thing about this particular brew was that it left his wits untouched. Well, mostly untouched. Perhaps a little touched. Her homespun robe was scrunching up and twisting.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Woman,\u8221\'94 Mandri said forcefully, trying to sound mean and sober, \u8220\'93I have made my decision\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 I have no interest in whatever job you\u8217\'92re offering. Now, this is my land and I did not invite you onto it. So, leave.\u8221\'94 He took another big swig of his hooch.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ohhh,\u8221\'94 Pel said, chuckling. \u8220\'93You }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 are}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  a delight. And the ox! Imagine my surprise to see him here. I do believe that this meeting of ours is fated.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri paled at the mention of fate. Nothing in this world was fated, not since Solraheen was killed. Then again, he couldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t trust his own view of things at the moment, because it took all the way up to now for him to notice that Vigram was also here. The big man stood some yards away, sweating in the sun like a dutiful servant. He stared at Mandri like he was planning to use Mandri\u8217\'92s bones to comb his beard.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked, just to make sure he wasn\u8217\'92t dreaming.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Vigram sneered, grunted, then eyed his fugitive ox with hate.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do not mind him,\u8221\'94 Pel said as she unfolded a short stool from nowhere. She sat down uncomfortably close, without asking. She she crossed her legs, again, without asking. She pulled a brown paper booklet from out of her ragged robe. This booklet had no cover, and its makeshift spine was composed of peeling wax and fraying twine.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She laid the booklet flat on her lap and flipped through a few pages. There weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t many of them. Mandri craned his neck to see if he could read what was there, but the burgeonsight made that impossible\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the pages oozed around her fingers.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So, you are Mandrameen,\u8221\'94 Pel said as she flattened an unruly page with her palm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What is happening here?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked. If that little book of hers was a book of debts, well, he would not be able to pay. And now he recalled the image of that decapitated head floating in Vigram\u8217\'92s leech lake. Then there was the matter of that dagger at Pel\u8217\'92s waist.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He took another swig of his beer.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel drew back her hood, revealing her bright, woodland eyes, and Sol\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s light, they sparkled under her piercing brow. She asked, \u8220\'93Do you still have your testicles?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Excuse me?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The girls in Meskalim all say that you are a eunuch.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri shook his head in disbelief. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Just because I don\u8217\'92t go in for the haggard women of Meskalim, that doesn\u8217\'92t make me a\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 wait, what is happening here?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Answer my question.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Of course I have my\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 hold on, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1\cf1 what}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  is happening here?\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93How tethered you are to your flesh?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri clutched his jug of hooch close. If this woman was after something, there wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t much she could take.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Ahhh!\u8221\'94 she exclaimed as she fingered something in her book. \u8220\'93Here you are. Mandrameen Andramawmun. That is your full name?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri nodded.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You have a good name,\u8221\'94 she said. \u8220\'93A strong Jandri name. An Old Jandri name. That is good. And my scribblings say you started learning how to paint at the age of five. Is that right? They say you were a decent painter.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri grimaced. Who was she to call him simply \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91decent\u8217\'92? And with that tone of voice? If this was a stab at his pride, he would not flinch. But, high as he was, his face might be doing things all its own. That was another thing about burgeons\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 it numbed the face but kept the muscles kept moving. He could be making kissy lips at her right now and he wouldn\u8217\'92t know it unless he focused.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It says you have a\u8230\'85\u8221\'94 Pel bent down to get a better look at the words. \u8220\'93It says you\u8217\'92ve a triptych hanging in the garden of Zee.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri nodded. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It is hanging inside the garden?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You couldn\u8217\'92t make it into the house?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri blanched at the insinuation. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re a right old hag, aren\u8217\'92t you? You know what? You can leave. I don\u8217\'92t care if you can turn me into a pig, I\u8217\'92m not going to paint anything for you. Vigram! A little more discretion with your mistresses, please!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m not here to commission you,\u8221\'94 Pel said through a smile. \u8220\'93I am here to tell you a story.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, you\u8217\'92re here to leave.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I will stay. I will stay because good stories are a valuable currency. They make people do things they would not otherwise think to do. In a way, the right story, applied to the right person, it buys them.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You want to buy me?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I want you to listen to my story about a wastrel. You know him.\u8221\'94 Her lips pinched into a wry grin, as if she\u8217\'92d just insulted Mandri and didn\u8217\'92t want to get away with it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Maybe I do,\u8221\'94 Mandri said flatly.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, I\u8217\'92m sure that you do. This wastrel, he was a\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri exaggerated a yawn, then settled into his tree like he was about to go in for another nap.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You might be interested to know that this is Vigram\u8217\'92s story,\u8221\'94 Pel said. \u8220\'93His true story. He never tells it because he believes it to be embarrassing. You see, he was like you, a wastrel. Pathetic. Stronger men than him were snuffed out in the womb. Truly, he was almost exactly as you are now. He stole his father\u8217\'92s money, the money meant to bring him up in this world, and he spent it on burgeons, women, and people who he thought were his friends. His life\u8217\'92s prospects quickly came to nothing.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri peeked an eye open, expecting to see Vigram explode in rage. Vigram was not a man who would assent to being talked about so cheaply. Yet, Vigram was silent, with arms crossed. He betrayed nothing of his mood except for the unease of having to stand in the sun.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93When Vigram\u8217\'92s father found out what he had done\u8230\'85oh my, the man\u8217\'92s anger was profound. His anger ran so deep that he began to believe that all of existence was a mistake. Given the rough nature of your current condition you might have some sympathy for that idea. Your life lacks purpose. I believe that I can go further and prove\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 beyond a shadow of a doubt\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 that we\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and I speak for every living creatures\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 we have no intrinsic purpose.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri looked to the ox, hoping the beast would counter the witch\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s opinion. Then he remembered that the ox could not speak the common tongue.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mandri,\u8221\'94 Pel said, regaining his attention. \u8220\'93Let me prove it.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Prove what?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I can prove that none of us are meant to be in this world. That means what purpose we have is crafted. It is made. It exists outside of ourselves. And as it is made, it is something we can lose.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What does this have to do with your story?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The loss of purpose is what grips the stomach of every story ever told. You see, Vigram\u8217\'92s father lost his purpose. This is the greatest horror a human being can experience, discounting extended bouts of torture. Don\u8217\'92t laugh, you of all people should know how loss can break a person.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t aware that he was laughing.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram, as a child, was his father\u8217\'92s purpose in life. To borrow a poetic turn, Vigram was his father\u8217\'92s challenge to the Black. Not only did the old man lose that challenge, his attempt was squandered. If Vigram were killed, he could have moved into grief, but no, he had challenged the Black and the Black answered back by turning young Vigram into the living embodiment of a life\u8217\'92s wasted labor. Inconsolable, the old man sent Vigram a note which said, \u8216\'91You are going to die\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Won\u8217\'92t we all.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes!\u8221\'94 Pel said excitedly. \u8220\'93Yes, you already see where this is going. Vigram might have been a wastrel, but he did not want to die. He read the note and panicked. He began to see his father in every shadow. He could not enjoy himself, could not bother the whores, you know, could not sleep in the same place twice. If you knew his father, you would know what was coming for him. What could he do? Vigram was a wastrel who could not confront even the thought of a confrontation. So, he ran away to find a witch to fix his problems for him. If a witch could predict the exact moment when his father would kill him, then he could, he hoped, avoid that fate. It took him a long while, but he found a witch. Guess what that witch did for him?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93She bored him.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93She showed him what he was.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93And what was that?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93A leech.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel reached over and ripped a leech off straight off the old ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s shoulder. Somehow, the ox didn\u8217\'92t seem to mind. It was caught in a sort of daze, still unrecovered from all that freeweed smoke, and likely, the loss of blood.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel held the leech up like a freshly caught fish. The black blob of a creature bunched up in her fist, then tried to stretch out of it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Disgusting thing,\u8221\'94 Mandri sneered.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Oh, please,\u8221\'94 Pel censured. \u8220\'93The leech is the only honest creature in all of creation. It doesn\u8217\'92t pretend to be more than what it is, which is a mouth, a stomach, and an anus. You, on the other hand, think that you\u8217\'92re a person.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Her eyes were fixed in a nasty squint.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Have you ever killed someone?\u8221\'94 she asked. Her voice was dire. The narrow whites of her eyes seemed possessed of an inner light.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Killing?\u8221\'94 Mandri mumbled. \u8220\'93What is this about?\u8221\'94 He looked to Vigram for reassurance, but the man was only grumpy about standing in the sun.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Answer my question,\u8221\'94 Pel insisted.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No, I\u8217\'92ve never killed anyone.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Would you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I guess it depends on who is getting killed,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. \u8220\'93Are these, I don\u8217\'92t know, are they murderers?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel frowned. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do not answer my questions with questions.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri reflexively took another drink. He swallowed hard. Pel seemed to grow taller in her seat. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Look,\u8221\'94 he said, after licking his lips, \u8220\'93What\u8217\'92s the point of this, anyway? You want me to kill someone?\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Would that be such a terrible a thing?\u8221\'94 Pel asked. \u8220\'93Right this very moment, people are dying. I do not mean one or two, their deaths number in the thousands. Thousands are dying, right this very moment. You do not think of them, but I do. It is my duty to ensure that they are dying for a purpose.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What do you need me for?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Have you ever prayed to Solraheen?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Who are you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s long nose appeared to lengthen. She tilted her head, and a lock of her brown hair turned silver. \u8220\'93Have you ever prayed to the good god Solraheen?\u8221\'94 she asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Of course I have, who hasn\u8217\'92t?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s upper lip thinned as it curled in contempt.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Have you spoken the words yourself?\u8221\'94 she asked. \u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t count prayer circles where someone else does the heavy lifting. Have you ever run away to a quiet place and whispered words to Solraheen?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri couldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t answer because the brown lines of Pel\u8217\'92s hair were fractaling into smoke. The heat from that smoke melted Pel\u8217\'92s clean eyebrows, causing them to drip down into her wrinkles\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 yes, wrinkles. She was no smooth beauty, Pel was a right old witch!}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re high as a blackbeak,\u8221\'94 Pel said as the rest of her youthful fat disappeared into her sunken cheeks. \u8220\'93That is good. You are fitting the mold. You are nearly untethered, that much is clear. However, I am not certain you will pass the test. Don\u8217\'92t look so frightened, boy, that also means that I am not certain you will fail. This should excite you. Are you excited?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re a witch!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel laughed. Hers was a grating laugh\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 half cackle, half delirious groan, as though cherishing Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s simplicity. There was nothing left of her youth in it. Still, she was a beauty in her own right, the kind which chastises instead of pleases. \u8220\'93Relax,\u8221\'94 she said. \u8220\'93I would never force you through the test if I thought the odds were against you. I do believe I see threads of Fate.\u8221\'94 She scribbled a little something in her book, closed it, then tucked it back into her robes. \u8220\'93From here, you do not get to decide whether or not you will play a part. If you are fit for divine purpose, then you will make the choice which is to be made, and that is that.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri whispered a chime of praise when she stood up, as he thought she was about to leave. Instead, she held him in her eyes. The burgeonsight had her green irises wiggling and popping like beans in hot oil.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What keeps you here?\u8221\'94 she asked. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No,\u8221\'94 Mandri said simply.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then you won\u8217\'92t mind if I make a meal of him?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Make a what?\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You won\u8217\'92t mind if I butcher and eat this ox?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why would I? It\u8217\'92s not mine.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s brow pinched in confusion. \u8220\'93Do you have no great feeling for this creature?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Does it have any feeling for me?\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel shook her head, apparently impressed with Mandri\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s apathy. Then she gripped the turtle-shell hilt at her waist and pulled a long, black knife from an unseen sheathe. The sudden sight of this thin, curved blade drew Mandri up, and when she bent over to put it against ox\u8217\'92s neck, he nearly fainted.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The boy fears death,\u8221\'94 Vigram said from his spot in the sun.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93He fears himself,\u8221\'94 Pel countered as she coaxed the ox into lifting its head. She scraped her knife\u8217\'92s edge up and down against its throat. The poor beast, eager to get in a good scratch, closed its eyes and lifted its chin as high as it could to make sure she could scratch the whole length of it. \u8220\'93Why would he fear death when he is the one with an insatiable stomach?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why not leave him alone?\u8221\'94 Mandri pleaded.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s right eye widened. She pulled her blade back from the ox\u8217\'92s neck. \u8220\'93Are you referring to you, or the ox?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Both?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Would you hurt me to save this ox?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri thought for a moment, suspecting a trap. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t think I could,\u8221\'94 he said. \u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re obviously beyond me. And Vigram\u8217\'92s right there. I\u8217\'92m as much at your mercy as the ox.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes, but if you had the power\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 if you could kill both of us to rescue this poor, suffering animal, would you do it?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri opened his mouth to say yes. Then he recalled how, moments ago, that ox had moseyed up to him looking for rescue, and he\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d done nothing. He couldn\u8217\'92t even bring himself to relieve it of a single leech when he could have easily done so. He could have done more than that, he have taken the ox over to\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You see!\u8221\'94 Pel exclaimed. \u8220\'93He hesitates!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Stern Vigram stroked his beard. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hesitation only means that he fears us.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes,\u8221\'94 Pel agreed. \u8220\'93But it could mean more as well. I do believe that there is room enough to put my finger on the balance.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 With uncanny speed she cupped Gil\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s snout under her arm and reached around his neck with her knife. She plunged her blade into the ox\u8217\'92s flesh, drove it tight up against its jaw, then abruptly yanked it round to the other side.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The ox let out a powerful bellow, deep and pitching. Its whole bulk lurched backwards, pulling its head from her grasp. It bucked as it stood up, knocking her aside, then ran off into the dusty field, bleeding and bleating as he went. It came to a stop just twenty yards away, apparently realizing there was nowhere to go. Confused at the betrayal, and weakened by the dreadful spurts of blood pouring out of its neck, it sank to his knees.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri snarled, picked up a rock, but one sidelong look from Pel melted his resolve.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Pathetic,\u8221\'94 she said with an awful sneer.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And then Mandri saw something he could not believe.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She flew.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pel flew. Feet off the dirt, straight like an arrow, she flew towards that dying ox. Shrouded in billowing black, she was nothing but an aged, scowling head, dark eyes alight with a bestial rage. The sound of her shriek was borne not from her lungs, but from the deepest depths of Shis.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 She plunged head first into the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s body, tearing a hole into its belly as easily as if her nose were cut from of flint. The ox crumpled to the ground, and she went with it, her head and shoulders fixed into its side. The ox\u8217\'92s spine spasmed as she tore herself free. The neck of its first stomach was clenched between her teeth. She spat it out, then ripped the rest of the innards free from their lining. Kneeling, she hugged the largest stomach up to the sun. Her lips uttered a speech as black as Fabled Black. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Mandri clasped his ears, but he could not look away. He prepared himself for some final gross barbarity, and it came when Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s chanting appeared to squeeze a bright speck of light from the ox\u8217\'92s stomach. She sipped this magnificence into her chest through pursed lips, as one might sip a too-hot tea.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Satiated, she dropped the great stomach onto the dirt.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The tension in her shoulders softened. Her face, a withered sponge of blood, smiled at Mandri. Then an unseen force plucked her off the ground and ripped her right out of the sky.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 When Mandri recovered his wits, she was gone without a trace, and Vigram was standing over him, excited.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93When she leaves like that,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, \u8220\'93it means that whatever happens next is what she wants to happen. A good sign. All you have to do now is get up and walk. Walk with me, Mandri. I should say that you don\u8217\'92t have a choice, but\u8230\'85well, I will say it: you don\u8217\'92t have a choice. Now, what do you want to do?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s face was scarcely legible. His beard was a blurry brown triangle. Blinding sunlight bounced off his bald head. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Money for burgeons,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93Five silver strings. Maybe more, enough money to buy out the whole valley. Stand up and walk with me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m not going anywhere.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Vigram chortled. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You most certainly are! You\u8217\'92ve already made the choice. Or I should say, you will make that choice. For once in your short life, you will overcome your fear, and your stubbornness, and you will give yourself a chance. You will accept the hand of fate when it reaches down to lift you out of this tangle that is your miserable life. Here. Here it is. You\u8217\'92ve been praying for it without knowing.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Vigram held out an uplifting hand.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 After a moment\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s indecision, Mandri took it, and let Vigram help him up.}}